<% content_for :own_heading, true %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
  <%= render "admin/component_demo/environment_banner" %>

  <%= admin_breadcrumbs(@breadcrumbs) %>

  <% description = "Two things on the grade book: an autosave label that moves too much, and a heading " \
                   "that does not read." %>
  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: "Save indicator", description: description } do %><% end %>

  <div class="space-y-10">
    <section data-testid="si-measured">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">1. How much it actually moves</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Measured by watching the element while editing: <strong>three text changes per edit</strong> -
        "Saving&hellip;", then "All changes saved", then a new timestamp. A 25-student book has about 100
        cells, so filling one in redraws that label roughly 300 times, each redraw pulling the eye to a
        place where nothing has happened.
      </p>
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-slate-700">
          ["Saving…", "All changes saved · 07:44 PM", "Saving…", "All changes saved · 07:44 PM", …]
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
          Two separate faults. The <strong>timestamp</strong> changes on every save and answers a question
          nobody asks - a teacher wants to know their work is safe, not the minute it was written. And
          <strong>"Saving…"</strong> flashes for a request that takes a few hundred milliseconds, so the
          transient state is on screen about as often as the steady one.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section data-testid="si-field">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">2. What the field does</h2>
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
        <dl class="space-y-2 text-sm">
          <div><dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Google Docs and Sheets</dt>
            <dd class="text-slate-700">One steady line in the toolbar - "All changes saved in Drive" -
              which does not tick. No per-save timestamp.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Notion</dt>
            <dd class="text-slate-700">"Edited just now" in the header, low contrast, coarse-grained.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Figma</dt>
            <dd class="text-slate-700">A small cloud icon. Text appears only while pending or on failure.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Linear</dt>
            <dd class="text-slate-700">Nothing at all. Saving is assumed.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
          The pattern is consistent: <strong>the steady state is quiet and does not change</strong>, the
          transient state is brief or suppressed, and nothing counts saves at the user.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section data-testid="si-option-a">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">A. Steady, and silent about the routine &mdash; recommended</h2>
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <span class="tw-btn-primary">Save grades</span>
          <span class="text-sm text-slate-600">All changes saved</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-slate-700">
          <li>No timestamp, ever. It is the churn, and "when" is not the question.</li>
          <li>"Saving&hellip;" appears only if a save is still running after <strong>800ms</strong>, so an
            ordinary edit changes nothing on screen at all.</li>
          <li>A failure says so and stays: <span class="font-medium text-red-700">Not saved &mdash; check
            your connection</span>. Nothing handles that today, and it is the only state a teacher has to
            act on.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
          Net effect while grading a class: the label is written <strong>once</strong>, not three hundred
          times.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section data-testid="si-option-b">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">B. Say nothing when it works</h2>
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <span class="tw-btn-primary">Save grades</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
          <strong>Rejected, narrowly.</strong> Figma and Linear do this and it is the quietest possible
          answer. But a teacher about to press a button that pays real money has a reasonable question -
          <em>is what I typed in there?</em> - and an empty space is the same thing an unsaved page shows.
          One quiet sentence answers it for the whole session.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section data-testid="si-option-c">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">C. Remove the Save button instead</h2>
      <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
          <strong>Rejected, and worth saying why.</strong> With save-on-blur the button is nearly
          vestigial, and Docs, Notion and Linear all ship without one. But it is also the <em>only</em>
          way to save if JavaScript does not run - autosave is a Stimulus controller - so removing it
          makes the page depend on JS to keep a teacher's work. It stays.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section data-testid="si-heading">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">3. The finalize card's heading</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        "Then finalize the quarter" does not read, and it is wrong twice: <strong>"Then"</strong> opens a
        heading with a sentence fragment, and <strong>"the quarter"</strong> names something the button
        does not - the button says "Finalize grades". Two names for one action is the drift this codebase
        keeps finding.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">Finalize grades <span class="text-slate-500">&mdash; recommended</span></p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Pays $12.40 into 2 students' portfolios and locks these entries. This cannot be undone.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">Uses your saved grades, above.</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
            The heading is the button's words, so the section and its control have one name. The sequence
            lives in the line beneath, where it was already doing the work - a heading is a label, not a
            step in a sentence.
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">Now finalize these grades</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
            The runner-up, and a real pattern: GOV.UK's check-your-answers page heads its final section
            "Now send your application". "Now" carries the sequence without the fragment. Worth taking if
            you want the order stated at the heading rather than under it.
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
          <p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">Pay these earnings <span class="text-slate-500">&mdash; rejected</span></p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
            Leads with the consequence, which is tempting for an irreversible action. But the control says
            Finalize, so this puts a third word on one thing, and "pay" reads like the button will move
            money the instant you press it rather than after a confirmation.
          </p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
